Pick it up.

691
00:37:37,130 --> 00:37:39,014
No baby you don't do that.

692
00:37:40,600 --> 00:37:42,250
Okay. Okay. Okay.

693
00:37:43,930 --> 00:37:44,750
Pick it up again.

694
00:37:47,610 --> 00:37:49,450
That's so cute. He keeps dumping it.

695
00:37:51,430 --> 00:37:55,450
Who's playing who? Is the question here.

696
00:37:55,930 --> 00:37:56,550
You know?

697
00:37:57,030 --> 00:38:00,360
So
babies love games.

698
00:38:01,120 --> 00:38:03,730
My son one time we went to you know in

699
00:38:03,740 --> 00:38:06,340
in the United States we have this store called Target.

700
00:38:07,320 --> 00:38:11,160
It's a religious obligation for Muslim women to go there and shop. It's a thing.

701
00:38:11,160 --> 00:38:16,060
So my wife was fulfilling her Wajib and she was taking, you know she was going and

702
00:38:16,060 --> 00:38:20,450
she made me come with her and you know at the time we only had two kids

703
00:38:21,020 --> 00:38:24,840
uh you know or two kids that were not in school. We were--we always have many kids.

704
00:38:24,840 --> 00:38:25,620
I lost count.

705
00:38:25,630 --> 00:38:30,040
But anyway so so we only had one son at the time and he was with us at the store.

706
00:38:30,720 --> 00:38:32,310
And he was still a young kid. He was a little guy.

707
00:38:32,470 --> 00:38:34,760
And so she said I'm gonna do my shopping whatever.

708
00:38:34,760 --> 00:38:38,230
So I went and you know, in the store, they have these giant light bulbs,

709
00:38:38,240 --> 00:38:39,720
they're light, but they're giant.

710
00:38:39,730 --> 00:38:40,740
They're bigger than the kid.

711
00:38:41,320 --> 00:38:44,220
So I was like, you know, keep the kid busy. So I put the ball down for him.

712
00:38:44,220 --> 00:38:47,960
So he's just, you know, he barely walks and he kicks it and he barely walks and

713
00:38:47,960 --> 00:38:52,620
then he kicks it and he barely walks and he kicks it and he's happy. He's just happy.

714
00:38:52,640 --> 00:38:57,340
So she's doing her shopping. And this kid goes all around Target and Target is huge.

715
00:38:57,920 --> 00:39:02,690
It's a massive, massive store and this kid is going and he's going and he's sweating,

716
00:39:02,700 --> 00:39:05,450
he's tired, but he needs to kick the ball

717
00:39:06,420 --> 00:39:10,740
and I'm not going to stop him. I'm just I mean this is no work for me.

718
00:39:11,420 --> 00:39:14,980
I was like, I don't have to pick him up. So let's let's ride this as much as we can.

719
00:39:15,000 --> 00:39:18,150
And he gets so tired at one point he just, he kicks

720
00:39:18,150 --> 00:39:20,450
it and he just falls on his bottom and he's sitting there.

721
00:39:24,220 --> 00:39:24,840
I'm coming.

722
00:39:25,720 --> 00:39:26,530
You know?

723
00:39:27,610 --> 00:39:28,330
And when he fell,

724
00:39:28,330 --> 00:39:30,450
I knew there's something wrong with the diaper too because you know,

725
00:39:30,450 --> 00:39:32,050
there's a kind of a soft fall.

726
00:39:32,710 --> 00:39:33,840
Look at that.

727
00:39:35,620 --> 00:39:39,250
It looks like mm hmm. Ok. Then I know, okay. Okay.

728
00:39:40,020 --> 00:39:43,140
So he's about to get up and kick it again. But his mother caught us,

729
00:39:43,820 --> 00:39:47,750
She's like, 'What are you doing? My poor baby, what what, why is he sweating so much?'

730
00:39:48,820 --> 00:39:53,030
And I said, 'No, he was just playing'
And the moment she picked him up

731
00:39:54,120 --> 00:39:56,680
he's gone. It's like, 'Oh my poor baby, you exhausted him.'

732
00:39:56,680 --> 00:39:58,010
I was like, 'No, he just wanted to play.'

733
00:39:58,010 --> 00:39:59,840
'That's all they want to do is they want to play.'

734
00:40:01,440 --> 00:40:05,460
But these kids, they get older and as they get older when they get like 13, 14,

735
00:40:05,460 --> 00:40:08,610
I'm really scared of that because my older daughter is 12, I'm really terrified.

736
00:40:08,620 --> 00:40:11,940
She's going to be a teenager. One of the ugliest creatures found in existence.

737
00:40:12,410 --> 00:40:15,910
You know, teenagers anyway, when they become teenagers,

738
00:40:15,920 --> 00:40:17,570
you know that that for boys,

739
00:40:17,570 --> 00:40:19,350
there's that crazy stage when they're not quite

740
00:40:19,350 --> 00:40:20,900
boys anymore and they're not really men.

741
00:40:20,900 --> 00:40:26,550
They're like half a mustache and they sound like they drank expired milk, like.

742
00:40:26,560 --> 00:40:28,130
You know, like,

743
00:40:28,610 --> 00:40:29,940
like that, that stage,

744
00:40:30,310 --> 00:40:31,240
it's really sad.

745
00:40:31,910 --> 00:40:32,630
But anyway,

746
00:40:33,510 --> 00:40:37,180
but they go through that age and at that age they really, really,

747
00:40:37,180 --> 00:40:38,480
really want to look good.

748
00:40:38,480 --> 00:40:38,710
Right?

749
00:40:38,710 --> 00:40:42,370
So all of a sudden before that when your kid is like five years old or seven years old,

750
00:40:42,380 --> 00:40:45,710
they don't match their socks, they put their shoes on backwards.

751
00:40:45,710 --> 00:40:48,090
They don't care what they're wearing or if they're not wearing anything.

752
00:40:48,090 --> 00:40:51,480
Like it's all good. 'Put your clothes on boy, put your clothes on.'

753
00:40:51,490 --> 00:40:52,020
You know?

754
00:40:52,410 --> 00:40:55,140
But afterwards what happens? They want to look good.

755
00:40:55,280 --> 00:40:58,140
'No, I don't want that one. That shirt doesn't match, no, no, no, I don't want that one.'

756
00:40:58,140 --> 00:40:58,790
'I want this one.'

757
00:40:58,960 --> 00:41:01,220
'Mom don't pick my clothes, I'll pick my own clothes.'

758
00:41:02,110 --> 00:41:04,060
You know, 'I want to get that.'

759
00:41:04,060 --> 00:41:06,500
'I want, I want to get those earrings, Mom, can I get my ears pierced?'

760
00:41:06,510 --> 00:41:08,780
You know my daughters, I didn't let them get their ears pierced.

761
00:41:08,780 --> 00:41:11,940
Their mother was begging me to let them get their ears pierced. I was like, 'No, not my girls.'

762
00:41:14,210 --> 00:41:16,030
'Put them fake things on, I don't care.'

763
00:41:16,910 --> 00:41:18,980
And she got them, got got them done when I was gone,

764
00:41:18,980 --> 00:41:22,030
I was in England and she got them pierced.

765
00:41:24,310 --> 00:41:24,820
It's like.

766
00:41:25,710 --> 00:41:26,520
But anyway,

767
00:41:27,010 --> 00:41:30,940
so you know, you get to a certain age and you start worrying about how you look,

768
00:41:31,310 --> 00:41:34,640
especially in the teenage years, you become really obsessed with how you look.

769
00:41:35,010 --> 00:41:38,440
And it's really funny because you constantly check yourself out.

770
00:41:39,310 --> 00:41:42,170
So when you're walking to the Masjid for Jumu'a and you see a teenager,

771
00:41:42,170 --> 00:41:43,440
it's the funniest thing.

772
00:41:43,810 --> 00:41:47,830
Teenagers love to check their own reflection in every car.

773
00:41:49,210 --> 00:41:50,640
So when they're walking they're like,

774
00:41:55,110 --> 00:41:57,440
It's awesome. It's it's so good.

775
00:41:58,510 --> 00:42:01,620
It's so good. Sometimes I go to a Masjid early.

776
00:42:02,300 --> 00:42:04,940
And stay in the car just to see what happens,

777
00:42:05,610 --> 00:42:06,320
you know?

778
00:42:07,700 --> 00:42:10,370
And even the guys, the guys that have like no hair,

779
00:42:10,370 --> 00:42:11,950
they're like they're less than my hair.

780
00:42:12,130 --> 00:42:14,320
He's got no hair. He's like a buzz, he's still going.

781
00:42:15,100 --> 00:42:19,420
Well, well you you can't fix this.

782
00:42:20,090 --> 00:42:23,640
Then there's girls who didn't wear hijab and they first start wearing hijab and

783
00:42:23,650 --> 00:42:26,860
you know they're still-- they're always worried that their hijab is not perfect.

784
00:42:26,860 --> 00:42:27,430
Like it's like

785
00:42:27,900 --> 00:42:29,720
higher over here and lower.

786
00:42:33,000 --> 00:42:34,430
You know?

787
00:42:35,100 --> 00:42:38,400
I used to think it's some kind of sign language then I realized there's a.

788
00:42:38,425 --> 00:42:40,110
I was like, 'There's a code?' [clicking sound]

789
00:42:40,900 --> 00:42:42,520
Like I don't know.

790
00:42:43,000 --> 00:42:43,810
But anyway

791
00:42:44,600 --> 00:42:46,070
so you're obsessed with your looks.

792
00:42:46,070 --> 00:42:47,900
Allah says worldly life is nothing but

793
00:42:47,900 --> 00:42:51,220
games and what's next? Beautification (وَزِينَةٌ).

794
00:42:51,230 --> 00:42:53,215
Then
وَتَفَاخُرٌۢ بَيْنَكُمْ

795
00:42:53,240 --> 00:42:57,430
Another stage hits you. Another stage in life where you want to show off to each other.

796
00:42:58,100 --> 00:42:59,650
Where you want to prove to each other.

797
00:42:59,650 --> 00:43:02,340
So when you go to a university and it's a prestigious university,

798
00:43:02,340 --> 00:43:04,800
you want to let people know that you go to that university.

799
00:43:04,810 --> 00:43:08,110
They didn't ask you. You just want to say it anyway. 'Hey where's the airport?'

800
00:43:08,110 --> 00:43:10,010
'It's over there by the way. I study at N. Y. U.'

801
00:43:11,500 --> 00:43:16,330
You know if you first- if you got a job and your job gives you a nice shiny badge I. D.

802
00:43:16,340 --> 00:43:17,740
You don't put it in your pocket.

803
00:43:17,740 --> 00:43:20,770
You stick it out even when you go like a friend's house. 'Hey what's that I.D.?'

804
00:43:20,770 --> 00:43:22,930
'Well you know it's my new job.'

805
00:43:24,400 --> 00:43:26,260
'Here's where they let me park my car. I took a picture.'

806
00:43:26,260 --> 00:43:28,510
'You wanna see a picture of where is my parking spot?'

807
00:43:29,100 --> 00:43:32,240
When you graduate out of school you get a diploma. What do you do with your diploma?

808
00:43:32,240 --> 00:43:33,620
You put it in the shelf?

809
00:43:34,000 --> 00:43:34,720
What do you do?

810
00:43:35,600 --> 00:43:36,650
You frame it on the wall.

811
00:43:38,000 --> 00:43:42,230
An age comes where you love telling people where you got your apartment,

812
00:43:42,800 --> 00:43:46,820
where where you got your job, what kind of car you bought.

813
00:43:47,500 --> 00:43:49,930
Because you want to let the people know that. 'Yeah man,

814
00:43:50,800 --> 00:43:51,430
I'm good.'

815
00:43:53,550 --> 00:43:54,920
But that age passes.

816
00:43:55,820 --> 00:43:57,840
That age passes and you stop worrying

817
00:43:57,840 --> 00:44:00,478
about showing other people. You don't care anymore.

818
00:44:00,502 --> 00:44:02,426
وَتَكَاثُرٌ فِى ٱلْأَمْوَٰلِ وَٱلْأَوْلَٰدِ

819
00:44:02,450 --> 00:44:05,420
The next stage hits you in life. The next stage is when

820
00:44:05,790 --> 00:44:08,100
you are just worried about getting enough

821
00:44:08,990 --> 00:44:09,710
of money.

822
00:44:10,490 --> 00:44:12,410
And having enough for your kids,

823
00:44:13,390 --> 00:44:14,710
you're just worried about the savings.

824
00:44:15,590 --> 00:44:17,460
You're just worried about 'We're still renting a house.'

825
00:44:17,460 --> 00:44:18,680
'When are we going to buy a house?'

826
00:44:18,690 --> 00:44:22,100
You're just worried about 'Do we have enough money to put our kids through school?'

827
00:44:22,790 --> 00:44:25,110
'How are we going to pay for college? How are we gonna do this?'

828
00:44:25,120 --> 00:44:25,940
'How are we going to do that?'

829
00:44:25,950 --> 00:44:29,210
You're constantly worried about gaining more and more and more .

830
00:44:29,210 --> 00:44:31,910
And all you talk about even with your friends are financial concerns.

831
00:44:32,790 --> 00:44:35,460
'Man, the expenses are getting high, how are we going do this?'

832
00:44:35,460 --> 00:44:37,130
'How what are you guys doing? How are we doing?'

833
00:44:37,260 --> 00:44:38,720
We're constantly talking about money.

834
00:44:39,780 --> 00:44:42,370
Money and expenses and money and expenses and kids.

835
00:44:42,550 --> 00:44:44,720
Those of you that have kids know exactly what I'm talking about.

836
00:44:45,390 --> 00:44:47,590
It just- it overtakes your mind.

837
00:44:47,614 --> 00:44:49,614
وَتَكَاثُرٌ فِى ٱلْأَمْوَٰلِ وَٱلْأَوْلَٰدِ

838
00:44:50,290 --> 00:44:53,470
This is Allah describing a summary of what people go through in life.

839
00:44:53,480 --> 00:44:54,410
It's very few words.

840
00:44:54,410 --> 00:44:58,420
But it's a very comprehensive summary isn't it? From childhood to adulthood.

841
00:44:59,290 --> 00:45:02,060
And then Allah gives an example of it. This is where the fun begins.

842
00:45:02,060 --> 00:45:03,040
The example of it is

843
00:45:03,064 --> 00:45:04,546
كَمَثَلِ غَیۡثٍ

844
00:45:04,570 --> 00:45:08,220
I can summarize, Allah says the entire worldly life that you enjoy.

845
00:45:08,245 --> 00:45:12,650
You can think of it like rain falling from the sky, perfect rain.

846
00:45:14,690 --> 00:45:18,070
The rain is so good that the farmer comes out of his house.

847
00:45:18,080 --> 00:45:21,020
You know the farmer puts seed in the ground and he was worried if it's

848
00:45:21,020 --> 00:45:24,510
going to rain or not. He doesn't know. He's praying for the rain to come.

849
00:45:24,520 --> 00:45:27,040
One morning he wakes up and it's perfect rain.

850
00:45:27,050 --> 00:45:30,570
He looks out the window and he notices that the brown soil,

851
00:45:30,580 --> 00:45:33,540
he sees little little little blades of green and

852
00:45:33,540 --> 00:45:35,950
that makes him really happy. By the way,

853
00:45:35,960 --> 00:45:38,850
when that first sign of green shows up in the farm.

854
00:45:38,860 --> 00:45:40,720
Is that the time to make the money?

855
00:45:41,090 --> 00:45:45,870
No. This little green has to grow all the way to look taller than the farmer,

856
00:45:45,880 --> 00:45:49,720
then he has to cut it, then he has to sell it, then he's going to make his money.

857
00:45:49,720 --> 00:45:52,900
Isn't that the case? But you know what none of that has happened yet.

858
00:45:52,910 --> 00:45:56,420
Just the thought of the green makes him happy in the Ayah.

859
00:45:56,890 --> 00:46:00,720
Just the green makes him happy and that's important because Allah is saying:

860
00:46:00,730 --> 00:46:03,970
It is not the acquisition of things that makes you and me happy.

861
00:46:03,980 --> 00:46:06,970
It is the thought of acquiring them that makes us happy.

862
00:46:06,980 --> 00:46:09,640
It is not necessarily marriage that made you happy.

863
00:46:09,640 --> 00:46:12,600
It is the thought of getting married that you were really happy about.

864
00:46:15,480 --> 00:46:16,700
"You get married?"
"Yeah" [laughs]

865
00:46:19,780 --> 00:46:21,440
The goofy smile on your face.

866
00:46:22,180 --> 00:46:27,940
You know, ask her, ask a girl who's about to get married in a couple of weeks. 'Hey, I saw

867
00:46:31,180 --> 00:46:34,710
like, are you okay there? You have sinus issues. What's going on?'

868
00:46:35,480 --> 00:46:38,990
Every time you say his name, she  goes.

869
00:46:39,780 --> 00:46:42,210
'That's not the case after marriage, we'll talk about that later.'

870
00:46:43,280 --> 00:46:45,355
But before.

871
00:46:45,380 --> 00:46:48,000
A couple of weeks before you graduate. Aren't you excited?

872
00:46:49,180 --> 00:46:52,900
It hasn't happened yet, but you're excited. You're not that excited after the fact.

873
00:46:53,280 --> 00:46:56,340
A couple of weeks before you watch the movie, 'Man, it's coming out.'

874
00:46:56,350 --> 00:46:57,630
'Oh, it's gonna be awesome.'

875
00:46:58,580 --> 00:47:01,210
Once you watch it all, 'That was that wasn't that great.'

876
00:47:02,880 --> 00:47:06,000
Kids; I do this experiment with my children all the time.

877
00:47:06,000 --> 00:47:08,730
My children are my favorite psychological test subjects.

878
00:47:09,880 --> 00:47:14,410
What I--my children are really into Legos, right? We're a Legos family. So

879
00:47:14,880 --> 00:47:18,000
I bought them a big giant thing of Legos.

880
00:47:18,880 --> 00:47:20,290
And I put it up on the shelf.

881
00:47:22,360 --> 00:47:23,980
'Can we play the legos?' 'No, not yet.'

882
00:47:25,380 --> 00:47:26,040
30 days.

883
00:47:27,980 --> 00:47:29,410
30 days. It's up on the shelf.

884
00:47:30,180 --> 00:47:31,710
As soon as you walk in the house you see the legos.

885
00:47:32,420 --> 00:47:37,750
'Baba can I can I just hold the box?' 'Yeah, you can hold the box.' 'Oh my God, this is so awesome.'

886
00:47:37,760 --> 00:47:40,060
And he's standing there and he's like shaking and he's,

887
00:47:40,070 --> 00:47:47,000
he's [sounds of excitement] with the box, with cardboard, he's so happy.

888
00:47:47,880 --> 00:47:49,120
For 30 days

889
00:47:49,120 --> 00:47:54,410
he was so happy that I let him open it and the happiness lasted about two days.

890
00:47:55,480 --> 00:47:58,030
Two days and it was worth it--it was worthless.

891
00:47:58,760 --> 00:47:59,400
Two days later.

892
00:47:59,980 --> 00:48:01,170
What is the Ayah telling us?

893
00:48:01,310 --> 00:48:03,090
The farmer gets really excited when?

894
00:48:04,470 --> 00:48:08,090
At the sight of progress, at the thought of acquisition.

895
00:48:09,570 --> 00:48:11,190
You know, before you buy a car,

896
00:48:11,870 --> 00:48:16,900
you look at the car, 'man, that's a sweet ride. Mm hmm I want to get that car.'

897
00:48:17,570 --> 00:48:18,700
'Such a nice car.'

898
00:48:21,770 --> 00:48:22,580
Then you buy the car

899
00:48:24,270 --> 00:48:25,240
and you're riding the car.

900
00:48:25,610 --> 00:48:27,000
You're really happy for about a week.

901
00:48:28,570 --> 00:48:30,000
And then the new model came out

902
00:48:32,160 --> 00:48:35,310
and the headlights that used to be like this are now like this.

903
00:48:36,070 --> 00:48:38,890
Which means your car is so ugly all of a sudden.

904
00:48:39,270 --> 00:48:42,840
'Why didn't they do that before? Why did they wait for me to buy this car?'

905
00:48:42,920 --> 00:48:45,900
'Then they turned it like that. Oh come on.'

906
00:48:47,270 --> 00:48:50,100
What is
the next phase? Allah says

907
00:48:50,993 --> 00:48:53,365
أَعْجَبَ ٱلْكُفَّارَ نَبَاتُهُۥ ثُمَّ يَهِيجُ

908
00:48:53,390 --> 00:48:58,000
Then the farmer sees that the crop matures.

909
00:48:59,270 --> 00:49:01,650
Now when the crop matures, what are you supposed to do?

910
00:49:02,870 --> 00:49:03,290
Cut it.

911
00:49:04,570 --> 00:49:08,250
But interestingly enough in this example, Allah says when the crop matures

912
00:49:08,275 --> 00:49:11,080
فَتَرَىٰهُ مُصْفَرًّا
You will watch it turn yellow.

913
00:49:11,470 --> 00:49:14,880
'What? I will watch it turn yellow. That's impossible.'

914
00:49:14,890 --> 00:49:17,080
If it turned yellow, it means it's gone bad.

915
00:49:18,370 --> 00:49:20,290
-It means I I- it's not- it's useless.

916
00:49:21,470 --> 00:49:25,090
'How could I have watched it turn yellow, like I lost interest in it.'

917
00:49:25,970 --> 00:49:28,340
The only way a farmer would not actually go out

918
00:49:28,340 --> 00:49:30,650
and cut the mature crop is if he lost interest.

919
00:49:30,650 --> 00:49:31,090
Isn't it?

